  "query_preview": "..\nA relatively calm period of isolated to scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms will persist through Wednesday with coverage\nhigher over Guam. Very light winds may allow island effect showers\nand isolated thunderstorms to develop for all islands, though likely\nnot too intense due to low level divergence and only moderate \nprecipitable water values. Westerlies over Guam may limit development\nor push any showers that develop offshore as was seen today. By\nWednesday, moist and unstable air from Invest 95W may allow for\nheavier island effect showers and isolated thunderstorms depending on\nupper level cloud coverage. Wednesday night through sometime \nThursday, the monsoon tail of 95W may bring numerous locally heavy \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms to the Marianas, with models\nindicating higher chances currently over Guam and Rota. Models\ncurrently show widespread 1 to 2 inches for this event but higher\ntotals will be possible in heavier, training showers. This will be\nmonitored closely over the next day in case a Hydrologic Outlook or \nFlash Flood Watch needs to be issued. Gusty winds will also be\nexpected with any thunderstorms. By late Thursday, dry conditions may\nreturn before a new monsoon surge potentially moves into the region\nover the weekend.",
